A handler that writes a timer's gate arms and clears the schedule.

`enabled` is not part of the schedule's identity, it is its *position*: html's
`timer` override places an `effect` under `if enabled`, so writing the var
changes the list of keys that position describes and the settle reconciles the
running schedule against it. Which means the write has to call the settle, and
nothing in the handler as written says so -- the same injection that patches the
button's label has to notice the gate too.

The regression is the tutorial "timers" lesson: clicking Start flipped
`running`, the label updated -- so the reactive updater *was* injected -- and the
timer never started, because the side effect of the gate changing was not.

Asserted as a golden rather than a grep for the sync call, because what matters
is that the settle is in the handler *and* what order it runs in relative to the
label update; a substring match sees neither.

deny out/none/html/index.html `\$timer_` -- html schedules through the effect settle, not a per-timer sync wrapper
deny out/none/html/index.html `setInterval\(.*\b1e3\b` -- the delay reaches setInterval as the effect's key, never inlined at the call

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import . "sngl:time"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

component main node {
    var seconds = 0
    var running = false

    timer(interval=1000ms, enabled=running, @tick { seconds += 1 })
    vbox {
        text(value="{seconds} s")
        button(text=running ? "Pause" : "Start", @click { running!! })
    }
}

window {
    main
}
